      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find out when all four tutors will next be together tutoring in the lab, we need to determine the next common day that they all fall on. This is equivalent to finding the least common multiple (LCM) of their working days – every 3rd school day, every 4th school day, every 6th school day, and every 7th school day.\n\nFirst, let's find the LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7. Each of these numbers has unique prime factors:\n- 3 is already a prime number.\n- 4 = 2^2\n- 6 = 2 * 3\n- 7 is already a prime number.\n\nThe LCM calculation will involve the highest powers of all prime factors involved.\n\nWe count the prime factors across the numbers using their highest prime occurrences:\n\n- 2 appears with the highest power of 2 (2^2 for 4, but we can ignore 4 since it's less than 3 = 2^1) \n- 3 appears with the highest power of 3 (3^1 for 3)\n- 7 appears with the highest power of 7 (7^1 for 7)\n\nThe LCM will be the product of the highest powers of all these primes:\nLCM(3, 4, 6, 7) = 2^2 * 3^1 * 7^1\n\nNow let's calculate it:\n- LCM(3, 2, 3, 7) = 2^2 * 3^1 * 7^1 = 4 * 3 * 7 = 84\n\nSo the LCM is 84 days from today. Therefore, all four tutors will next be together tutoring in the lab in 84 school days from today.",
