      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Willem van der Kooij\nTo find out on which day the tutors will be together again, we must find the least common multiple (LCM) which is something we are good at.\n\nFirst of all, let's list out the first few multiples for each tutor as follows:\n\nWanda: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 36, 40 ...\n\nDarren: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36, 39 ...\n\nBeatrice: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, 42, 48, 54, 60 ...\n\nChi: 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49, 56 ...\n\nWe can now write these as common multiples of these 4 numbers as follows:\n\nWanda, Darren, and Beatrice : 12 \nWanda, Darren, and Chi : 42\nWanda, Xi, Chi, and Beatrice: 84\nWanda, Adrienne, Chi, and Beatrice : 168\nWanda Adrienne, Chi, Beatrice : 240\nWanda Adrienne, Chi, Beatrice, and Darren : 336\nWanda, Adrienne, Chi, Darren, and Beatrice: 420\n\nTherefore, the next time they will all be together, it will be in 420 days (from today)\n\n$\\boxed{420}$",
